WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
Back in June at AI

2
00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:04.880
DevCon in London, a lot of speakers said a version of the same thing.

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.840
The hard part isn't writing code.

4
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.600
It's proving that it does what you asked.

5
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.040
Let's first hear from Shachar Azriel, VP of Product at Baz,

6
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.720
who wrote a spec, attached a recording of the exact bug

7
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.520
he didn't want, repeated, and got that exact bug back.

8
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.320
Part of what we wanted to do

9
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.560
is to add this to the onboarding flow of the product.

10
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.120
So when a user is onboarding to Baz, they will have the option to integrate

11
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.200
whichever of the ticketing systems available that they have.

12
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.800
And because I'm reviewing every product, every feature that is being released,

13
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.320
I asked to make sure that the same bug

14
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.160
that happened when we integrated another ticketing system won't repeat again.

15
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.600
I literally added a recording from a previous time

16
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.400
when we added another integration where the continue button

17
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.520
was overlapping the new integration that was added

18
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.360
a couple of days later, the developer sent me a slack

19
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.640
message telling me, hey, I just completed that feature.

20
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.160
Here's the link to Preview Environment.

21
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.040
Please review it. Guess what?

22
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.400
The continue button is

23
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.400
exactly where I asked him not to put it.

24
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:19.880
Guys.

25
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.040
Like, I couldn't be more explicit than that.

26
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.320
And I'm showing this because this is a really simple example, right?

27
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:34.280
Like, for those of you who are part of an engineering team?

28
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:35.600
Product, design.

29
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.680
This is our day to day frustration.

30
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.280
We're writing specs.

31
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.480
They are very detailed and we're very happy

32
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.680
to deliver them to the engineering teams that we're working with.

33
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.680
But unfortunately when we see the results is not as we expected.

34
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.360
And you can understand that if this is such a front-end

35
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.520
simple task that's gone wrong,

36
00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.160
how like what would be the results if we're doing a complex task?

37
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.880
So the bottom line is that coding agents that we are using

38
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.120
are not focused on verifying features.

39
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.920
They are amazing at generating code.

40
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.080
And I think one of the themes that is repeated

41
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.320
in a lot of the talks here and in other conferences is the fact that

42
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.640
it's so easy to build right now, but the way that the coding agents are built,

43
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.440
they are focused on generating features.

44
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.720
They are not focused in fully extracting all the specs that we have, all the specs

45
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.760
that we write, and they are not focused in verifying the feature as it is.

46
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.960
We also heard from Justin Cormack, formerly CTO at Docker,

47
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.240
who wrote 350,000 lines of Rust with agents.

48
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.280
He tried the advice about chasing 100% test coverage,

49
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.400
and found the agent writing tests for things that cannot happen.

50
00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.760
What actually pinned the behavior down was pointing his tests at the real thing.

51
00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.240
I keep hearing people say, well, with AI, all you need is 100% test coverage

52
00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.040
and you're done. And it's like,

53
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.000
so but I did try that.

54
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.960
I started off trying to get 100% test coverage.

55
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.280
It seemed like a good idea.

56
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.880
And I also measured different measures of 100% test coverage

57
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.880
from test coverage, from integration tests.

58
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.240
And it didn't

59
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.960
really didn't really help a lot.

60
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.080
I found they were kind of better uses of my time than trying to get 100%.

61
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.200
You know, I sat down and I asked an AI agent to get 100% test coverage.

62
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.000
It kind of wrote trivial tests that were like,

63
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.080
I don't, you know, this is a stupid test.

64
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.640
I don't care about that.

65
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.680
And there's

66
00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.280
also a lot of weird error cases that are really hard to cover.

67
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.240
I mean, I went through and asked the agent,

68
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.440
like, well, why haven't we got 100% coverage from this?

69
00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.360
It was like,

70
00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:00.000
we don't have a test for when the random number generator in the system fails.

71
00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.400
And I was like, yeah, I don't think I want to write a test for that.

72
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.520
Like, that's just too much error injection.

73
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.720
Like I can see that there's a, you know, it's going to it's going

74
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.000
to give an error or it's going to panic and that's the right behavior.

75
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.800
I don't need to have test coverage for that on all my test suite.

76
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.080
But I still have a lot of tests.

77
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.000
I mean, 75% of my code base, test

78
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.480
coverage is between 75 and 100% for each file.

79
00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.600
Like, it's like you can still have a lot of tests

80
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:36.240
without being really obsessive, about 100% test coverage,

81
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.400
which I think is not really the right kind of aim.

82
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.880
The great thing about

83
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.640
copying an existing system like S3 is you've got this test oracle.

84
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.520
You can find out what happens when you do something.

85
00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.640
You run the test, you can run it against the you can run it in my case against S3,

86
00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:59.000
and I have 1500 tests that run against S3 and they lock down the behavior.

87
00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.080
Ian Thomas at Meta ran workshops across the company

88
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.440
and found the same worry coming back from nearly every team.

89
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.920
Can we trust the code?

90
00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:12.440
Their answer was an anti test slop initiative,

91
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.400
using AI to judge whether the AI's tests are worth having.

92
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.320
One of the things in Meta that we use a lot is called Workplace,

93
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.440
which is essentially an internal version of Facebook.

94
00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.880
So every time anyone ran one of these assessments,

95
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.640
they would post the results and they would share the insights they'd uncovered.

96
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.600
And so it was a case of everybody else would then

97
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.720
get visibility into what other teams were doing, raising up the network effect.

98
00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.240
One of the big things that came out from pretty much

99
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.360
every single workshop was, can we trust the code?

100
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.800
And it was one of the big reasons

101
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:45.200
of the senior engineers was slightly hesitant about adopting

102
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.520
AI tooling in the first place. They wanted to have control.

103
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.400
They liked finessing what they were doing.

104
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.080
They like to feel that they had the craft in hand.

105
00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.480
And that was great because it told us where we could focus on

106
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:01.240
more cross-cutting efforts as a leadership group, a team that were looking to help

107
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:02.680
the adoption curve.

108
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:07.680
We could find ways to to unilaterally improve people's experiences.

109
00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.280
So on the back of things like we were, we were looking at testing

110
00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.000
and how we could increase our test coverage

111
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.400
and reduce flakiness, to Justin's comments before.

112
00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:23.000
Well, we were seeing that AI was great at hallucinating awful tests.

113
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.920
It would make stuff that was really looking like

114
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.600
it was doing something useful, but it really wasn't,

115
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.920
and it was just increasing our overhead on CI.

116
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.360
So we invested in an anti test slop initiative, where again, on CI,

117
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.400
we could go and validate the changes that were being made in an autonomous

118
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.680
way, using a different AI tool to judge the quality of the output.

119
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:44.880
And these things are, again, examples of the ground-up culture that we have

120
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.920
is that this thing started out in Horizon, but the test slop

121
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.960
initiative that we started and the tooling that was built

122
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.600
was eventually adopted by infra and rolled out across the whole company.

123
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.640
And I think to date, it still runs pretty much on every diff.

124
00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:01.000
That's got significant volume of AI generated tests in it.

125
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.080
And to close, Christopher Batey on what you do

126
00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.400
when a 7,000-line pull request lands.

127
00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.440
His answer is to stop pretending you can review it and move the human decision.

128
00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:17.240
Earlier. Architecture decisions get their own reviewed pull request.

129
00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.560
After that, agents are very good at checking code against them.

130
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.920
Now, I could just describe the feature I want, the feature I want

131
00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:27.880
which would combine this,

132
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.640
and I'm pretty confident that our agentic workflow would build it.

133
00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:35.080
And then maybe a pull request is going to land, which is maybe

134
00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.120
7,000, 7,000 changes.

135
00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.840
And then what would I be reviewing it for?

136
00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.520
Well, I first want to be reviewing it for the systems thinking.

137
00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.800
So here's some questions which I just threw together

138
00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.160
about what I'd want to know about this change.

139
00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.000
How does the client side connect to the managed service?

140
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.160
What does it connect to?

141
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.760
How does it authenticate and authorize?

142
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.320
Because this is a client side component.

143
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:57.840
Talking to a multi-tenant managed service, what parts of the managed service

144
00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:58.920
are exposed to the internet?

145
00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.080
We're very careful about not exposing things.

146
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.560
You know, do we ever share an API which is used by a human-usable front end

147
00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.360
versus like an agent system?

148
00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.520
What data can pass from the client to the managed?

149
00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.720
What data can pass from the managed to the client?

150
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.880
There are different deployment mechanisms, right?

151
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.280
We do continuous delivery virtually every commit for the managed service.

152
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.960
But the client side, that means that we need

153
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.240
we need to do something on our client.

154
00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.840
And maybe that might get updated once a week because it's deployed

155
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.280
to their systems.

156
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.760
So that's the type of thinking I definitely want to keep

157
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.080
in the engineers that I work with.

158
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.600
So one thing we do now is when we're

159
00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.520
planning whatever work we're going to do in the next week

160
00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:34.760
is if

161
00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:38.520
we think there's a system-level decision that always comes as a completely separate

162
00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.480
pull request in something called an architectural decision record.

163
00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.240
If you've not come across those, Google them.

164
00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.360
It's about documenting your technical decisions

165
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.280
along with your code so that you can.

166
00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:48.640
Someone can quickly learn

167
00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:51.880
why we made the decision, so we don't keep on discussing them again.

168
00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:55.560
And we've always used ADR, or at least for a very long time.

169
00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:56.680
And we've always used ADRs, or at least for a very long time.

170
00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.680
agentic development, all of the agents loved writing ADRs.

171
00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.360
They were very good at seeing that we had ADRs.

172
00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.400
So they wrote more ADRs, and our ADRs got longer and more verbose.

173
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.560
And not a single human understood them, which completely defeated the point

174
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:08.280
of having them.

175
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.040
So what we did is we said from now on, we're going to start with the ADR.

176
00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.000
I, me as a human, I'm going to spend a lot of time reviewing it.

177
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.680
I want diagrams, I want them to be visual so I can understand them.

178
00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.520
And then humans reviewing them can really put a lot of effort

179
00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.800
into reviewing those architectural decision records.

180
00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.600
And once you've got those agents are really, really good

181
00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:28.280
at reviewing

182
00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:32.160
implementations against well-structured architectural decision records,

183
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:33.760
not only when you eventually land

184
00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.160
the implementation, but all of the follow up requests then.

185
00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:37.880
Because one thing

186
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.720
I don't think humans are good at is keeping all of those ADRs in your head,

187
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:42.080
and then

188
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:45.840
subsequent pull requests, which subtly, perhaps change your system architecture.

189
00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:47.600
You might not realize that something's happened.

190
00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.720
Something's happening like a new API is exposed.

191
00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:50.960
But what we actually do is

192
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.800
have the ADRs constantly reviewed against all of the pull requests that come

193
00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.200
Thanks for watching.

194
00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:01.840
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

195
00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.800
Visit AI DevCon to learn more and book your ticket.
